+{"sections":[{"topic":"authority","conclusion":"The authority model enforces strict ownership boundaries through durable AgentDirectory projection. Main agents and investigation children have separate permissions: main agents require caller binding verification (line 45-48), while investigation children must be direct owned descendants (line 109-112). Workspace versioning is handled through event store identity comparison in durable_log_identity() to ensure operations target the correct workspace revision. History inheritance is enforced by requiring forked_from_session_id to be None for investigations, ensuring clean lineage.","evidence":[{"path":"src/traceh/supervision/authority.py","start_line":45,"end_line":48},{"path":"src/traceh/supervision/delegation.py","start_line":109,"end_line":112}]},{"topic":"budget","conclusion":"Budget reservation follows a strict three-phase pattern: reserve, admit, and settle. Subtasks reserve budgets before creation (line 128-132) to prevent over-allocation, while the main agent retains a 'wall reservation' for cleanup operations (line 265-270). The budget system uses append-only ledger facts with CAS operations to ensure consistency, and reserves capacity for cleanup by maintaining separate wall-time reservations that are not consumed by regular work.","evidence":[{"path":"src/traceh/budgets/service.py","start_line":128,"end_line":132},{"path":"src/traceh/budgets/enforcement.py","start_line":265,"end_line":270}]},{"topic":"lifecycle","conclusion":"Lifecycle management ensures no running work remains through coordinated admission leases and disposal scopes. Creation/cancellation uses the AgentLifecycleCoordinator's admission context manager (line 102-105) to prevent concurrent operations, while cleanup failures are handled by the dispose() method's idempotent implementation (line 120-123). Products are captured during the settlement phase when usage facts are committed to the ledger, ensuring durable evidence of completed work.","